Glibc 2.35’s tcache (thread-local `malloc`-managed cache) holds freed chunks in a singly-linked list indexed by size. An attacker fills the tcache for a 0x20-byte chunk (`tcache->entries[0x20] = victim_chunks`), then frees a controlled pointer `A` into the same bin. If `A` overlaps a valid chunk `B`, the `fd` field of `B` can overwrite the `tcache->entries[0x20]` pointer.

Exploit shellcode:

```
void *t[7];                // fill tcache
t[0]=malloc(0x20); free(t[0]);
t[1]=malloc(0x20); free(t[1]);
// ... until t[6] = fake fd pointing to _IO_list_all-0x23
t[7]=malloc(0x20);
```

The UAF reallocates `t[0]` to point inside `main_arena`, corrupting `global_max_fast` or `mp_` structures. ASLR bypass uses `leak libc via __free_hook → system()` or via `dl_fini` in a 64-bit address space.

Mitigation: glibc 2.36 re-enables `tcache->key` canary (`TCACHE_KEY`) ensuring list integrity.

